// Practice quiz: Calling another service with RestClient: base URL and timeouts
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/integration-and-observability/calling-another-service-with-restclient-base-url-and-timeouts

::o1 builder::Why inject RestClient.Builder rather than calling RestClient.create? {
~RestClient.create does not exist#Not quite. It exists, but skips these settings.
=The builder picks up Boot auto-configuration#Correct. Converters and global settings apply.
~The builder removes the need for a URL#Not quite. You still give a URL.
~It is required for GET#Not quite. Both can GET.
}

::o1 base url::Where should the partner base URL live? {
~Hardcoded in the method#Not quite. That is hard to change.
~In the JSON response#Not quite. That is not where it comes from.
~In a test only#Not quite. Production needs it too.
=In configuration, so that each environment can differ#Correct. Then no rebuild is needed to change it.
}

::o2 timeout exception::What does a read timeout surface as with RestClient? {
=ResourceAccessException#Correct. It wraps the I/O failure.
~HttpClientErrorException#Not quite. That is for a 4xx response.
~NullPointerException#Not quite. That would be a bug.
~No exception at all#Not quite. A timeout raises an exception.
}

::o2 mapping::A partner 500 is not your client fault. Which answer from your API fits best? {
~200#Not quite. That hides a failure.
~400#Not quite. The client request was fine.
=502#Correct. Bad gateway says the upstream answered badly.
~201#Not quite. Nothing was created.
}

::o3 test tool::Which tool tests a RestClient-based class with no network? {
~A real partner service#Not quite. That needs a network.
~@DataJpaTest#Not quite. That tests persistence.
=MockRestServiceServer#Correct. It answers at the client layer.
~Flyway#Not quite. That runs migrations.
}

